.TH CHTTPCLI_SET_ENGINE_LOGGER 3 "2026-07-14" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpcli_set_engine_logger \- set the logger for the diagnostics of the async engine of chttpclient
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpclient.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpcli_set_engine_logger(clog cl);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function derives a child logger from cl with the extra field
component=http-client-engine, and installs it as the diagnostics
logger of the shared async engine behind Tier 2 and Tier 3. If an
engine logger was installed before, it is closed.

You can call this function at any time, and it takes effect at once.
The engine keeps the child logger across every stop and restart until
another call to this function replaces it.

chttpclient_do_async, chttpclient_do_async_streaming and the
pooled-sync wrappers built on them (chttpclient_do_pooled and
chttpclient_do_pooled_streaming) share one ccol_event_loop reactor.
That reactor serves the whole process and every chttpcli instance in
it, the default client included, and the library starts it the first
time it is needed. This function captures that reactor's diagnostics,
such as a failed TLS handshake or a connect error. It is separate from
the logger of each client, and from the reactor of
chttpsvr_set_engine_logger as well: chttpserver and chttpclient each
own a fully independent static reactor, and one process can run both
at the same time.

While no logger from this function is installed, the library opens a
fallback logger every time the engine starts and closes it when the
engine stops. The fallback writes to fd 2 at level CLOG_FATAL, and
this engine never logs its own diagnostics above CLOG_INFO, so the
fallback stays silent until you install a more talkative logger with
this function.

The caller keeps ownership of cl and may close it once this call
returns, because the child logger shares cl's destination and clogger
keeps that destination open while any logger that shares it is open.
The engine owns the child logger, and the caller must not close it.
The library closes it when another call replaces it, and at process
exit or library unload if no engine is running at that point.

For a logger from clog_create_from_fd or clog_create_from_fd_mp, that
destination is the logger's state and not the file descriptor: such a
logger never owns its fd, and no clog_close closes it; see
clog_create_from_fd(3). The engine keeps writing to that fd after you
close cl, so the fd itself must stay open until another call to this
function replaces or releases the engine logger, or until the process
exits.

Calling it with cl equal to CLOG_INVALID releases the installed engine
logger. The engine closes its child logger, and every later start of
the engine uses the fallback logger again. While the engine runs, the
release is refused with ccol_not_permitted because the engine logs
through that logger, so destroy every client and call
chttpcli_engine_wait first. Once a release has returned ccol_success,
the engine makes no further call through that logger, and its
destination, its file descriptor and cl's allocator may go away.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, if the call succeeds.
ccol_not_enough_memory, if the library cannot allocate the child
    logger.
ccol_not_permitted, if cl is CLOG_INVALID and the engine runs.
ccol_invalid_args, if cl is not CLOG_INVALID but is closed or stale;
    nothing changes, and the engine keeps the logger that it had.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpcli_engine_wait (3),
.BR chttpcli_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs (3),
